In article <3478.27359c0c@cc.nu.oz.au> c8730752@cc.nu.oz.au writes:
>>I would like so see( this must have been talked about before), a "hunger
>>meter" and a "weight meter". Sometimes you are asked if you want to stop
>>eating sometimes you aren't! The present indicator of hunger is quite 
>>difficult to judge, unless you start timing your steps!

I agree

>
>There is a "hunger meter".  If you are not satiated when you eat, you will
>not choke.

This is a very coarse grain meter...  There is no way to tell that in a
couple of moves time you'll be hungry and regret not having eaten that
slightly dodgy ettin corpse left by the zombie....

>
>There is a "weight meter".  If you pick up something when close to your
>maximum, you will get told that you have a little trouble lifting it.
>

This is not a "meter" - more like a warning bleep - there is no way to
tell that you are still lightly enough loaded to go through narrow gaps
without actually trying to do it and no way to tell if you can lift the
treasure chest without actually attempting it.

A useful meter would show your current state relative to the points of
interest and give some feedback on current capacity or need.

However, although I would like meters I would not want them at the
expense of complicating the interface - I find it hard enough to keep an
eye on my hitpoints as well as read the relevant messages that have
appeared - the game might not be real-time but I often find myself
reacting and playing as though it is.....

Great game!

